// // STRATEGY design pattern: defines a family of algorithm, put them into different classes so that they can be  changed at run time 

// eg: suppose we have a parent robot class with walk and talk methods along with an abstract projection method, and the children are companion robot and worker robot (they defined their own projection method), but lets say now have to add more types robots having ability to fiy and now we have to define the fly method in each of the children class and it will break the DRY principle, to solve this now we have to just make a child class - flying class that inherits the robot class and has defined fly method, now if we have some more robots that fly but differntly then we can overwrite the fly method but that will again break the DRY principle, so these inheritance problem can go on foreve

// Solution: we create separate interfaces for walk, talk, fly etc and declare methods in them and then create multiple classes according to the different behaviours required, and now the main robot class stores the references of these interfaces and then during robot object creation whatever type of behaviour object is passed that will be used in that object, in this approach the robot calss is know as client and the interfaces are called strategies and the concrete classes of those interfaces are called concrete strategies